---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { path } from '../../lib/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const updated = '6 August 2026';
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  routeKey="cookies"
  title="Cookie policy | enable.advisory"
  description="enable.advisory cookie policy: this site uses no profiling cookies and no analytics tools, only strictly necessary technical ones. Explained in plain language."
  ogImage="/og/cookies-en.png"
>
  <Breadcrumbs lang={lang} items={[{ name: 'Cookie policy', path: path('cookies', lang) }]} />

  <!-- ================================ HEADER ================================ -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap pt-12 pb-14 md:pt-16 md:pb-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="label-caps text-mist" data-reveal>Legal</p>
        <h1 class="font-display text-h2 mt-6 font-medium" data-reveal>Cookie policy</h1>
        <p class="mt-8 text-lead text-mist">
          This page is short because the site is simple. As of today we use no profiling cookies, no
          advertising pixels and no analytics tools: we don’t measure you, and we don’t follow you
          anywhere else.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-8 text-[0.8125rem] text-mist">Last updated: {updated}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================= BODY ================================= -->
  <section class="hairline">
    <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <!-- 1 -->
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-display text-h3 font-medium" data-reveal>What cookies are</h2>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mist">
            A cookie is a small text file a website stores in your browser and can read back on your
            next visit. Other technologies — localStorage, pixels, SDKs, fingerprinting — do similar
            things under different names, and the law treats them the same way.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-5 text-mist">
            Broadly, there are two families. <span class="text-paper">Technical cookies</span> make
            the site work or deliver something you actively asked for: remembering a preference,
            keeping a session open, protecting a form from automated submissions.
            <span class="text-paper">Profiling and measurement cookies</span> count, recognise and
            reconstruct your interests, often on behalf of third parties — those are the ones the
            consent banner exists for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- 2 -->
        <div class="hairline mt-12 pt-12">
          <h2 class="font-display text-h3 font-medium" data-reveal>
            What this website actually uses
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mist">As of the last update shown above:</p>
          <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-mist">
            <li>
              <span class="text-paper">No profiling cookies</span> and no advertising pixels, from
              any platform.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="text-paper">No analytics tools</span> — no Google Analytics and no
              alternative either, including the ones that call themselves privacy-friendly.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="text-paper">No embedded third-party components.</span> Typefaces are
              served from our own domain rather than loaded from an external service, and there are
              no videos, maps, chat widgets or social embeds opening connections to other sites while
              you browse.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="text-paper">Strictly necessary technical cookies, if any</span>, set by
              the infrastructure serving the pages or by the service receiving contact form
              submissions, for security and spam protection. [PLACEHOLDER: list the technical cookies
              actually set by the hosting/CDN and by the form provider — name, purpose and duration;
              if none are set, state that explicitly here.]
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mist">
            If you fill in the contact form, the data you enter is processed as described in our
            <a href={path('privacy', lang)} class="link-plain text-paper">privacy policy</a>. Two
            different things: that one covers the data you send us, this one covers the files left in
            your browser.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- 3 -->
        <div class="hairline mt-12 pt-12">
          <h2 class="font-display text-h3 font-medium" data-reveal>
            Why there is no consent banner
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mist">
            A banner is required when a site installs tools that need your prior consent. For
            strictly necessary technical cookies, Article 122 of the Italian Privacy Code
            (Legislative Decree 196/2003) and the Garante’s cookie guidelines require information,
            not consent — and information is exactly what this page provides.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-5 text-mist">
            Showing you a banner you don’t need would be theatre. For a firm that works in
            compliance, it would also be an odd way to demonstrate competence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- 4 -->
        <div class="hairline mt-12 pt-12">
          <h2 class="font-display text-h3 font-medium" data-reveal>
            Managing cookies in your browser
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mist">
            Banner or no banner, control stays with you. Every browser lets you inspect stored
            cookies, block them in whole or in part, and delete them: the instructions live in the
            privacy and security settings of Chrome, Safari, Firefox and Edge. Private browsing
            windows clear everything on close.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-5 text-mist">
            One caveat: blocking technical cookies indiscriminately can stop some functions — form
            submission, for instance — from completing. On this site, the content itself stays
            readable either way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- 5 -->
        <div class="hairline mt-12 pt-12">
          <h2 class="font-display text-h3 font-medium" data-reveal>If anything changes</h2>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mist">
            This page describes a situation, not a permanent promise. If we ever add measurement or
            marketing tools, this page gets updated before they go live, not afterwards.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-5 text-mist">
            [PLACEHOLDER: update this page if analytics, pixels or marketing tools are added, and
            activate a compliant consent banner — scripts blocked until a choice is made, granular
            consent per purpose, refusing as easy as accepting, and the ability to change your mind
            at any time.]
          </p>
          <p class="mt-8 text-[0.9375rem] text-mist">
            Questions about this page or our
            <a href={path('privacy', lang)} class="link-plain text-paper">privacy policy</a>?
            <a href={path('contact', lang)} class="link-plain text-paper">Write to us</a> — a person
            answers, within one business day.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
